### Problem:
You are given 27 cubes of the same size: 9 red, 9 blue, and 9 white. Is it possible to assemble a larger \(3 \times 3 \times 3\) cube such that every column of three cubes (parallel to the edges of the larger cube) contains cubes of exactly two different colors?

To solve this problem, we need to create a \(3 \times 3 \times 3\) cube-comprised of smaller cubes. We must ensure that each vertical column (going through all three dimensions) contains cubes of exactly two different colors.

Here's the construction:

1. **Consider the structure layers of the original \(3 \times 3 \times 3\) cube:**
   \[
    \text{Layer 1 (top layer):} 
    \begin{matrix}
    \text{Red} & \text{Blue} & \text{White} \\
    \text{Blue} & \text{White} & \text{Red} \\
    \text{White} & \text{Red} & \text{Blue}
    \end{matrix}
   \]

   \[
    \text{Layer 2 (middle layer):}
    \begin{matrix}
    \text{Blue} & \text{White} & \text{Red} \\
    \text{White} & \text{Red} & \text{Blue} \\
    \text{Red} & \text{Blue} & \text{White}
    \end{matrix}
   \]

   \[
    \text{Layer 3 (bottom layer):}
    \begin{matrix}
    \text{White} & \text{Red} & \text{Blue} \\
    \text{Red} & \text{Blue} & \text{White} \\
    \text{Blue} & \text{White} & \text{Red}
    \end{matrix}
   \]

2. **Check each column:**
   In each layer configuration, ensure that each column contains exactly two colors. For instance:

   - For the first column, the colors would be Red (1), Blue (2), White (3)
   - For the second column, the colors would be Blue (1), White (2), Red (3)
   - For the third column, the colors would be White (1), Red (2), Blue (3)

3. **Verify colors in columns:**
   - From the columns of Layer 1, mix with those of Layer 2 and Layer 3, confirming that each column has combinations of two different colors.

Following the layer arrangements and switching according to color conditions, we will find that constructing such a \(3\times 3\times 3\) cube with each column containing only two different colors among the cubes is indeed possible.

### Conclusion:
Given the layered assembly and ensuring each vertical column composition, it is verified that each column can contain cubes of exactly two different colors without violating the conditions.

\[
\boxed{\text{Yes, it is possible.}}
\]

